  "full_review": "The would-be comic thriller “Eight Legged Freaks” (Warner Bros.) takes place in a dying Arizona town where giant spiders are stomping the population. Overacted and underwhelming, it shouldn’t cause a stampede to the box office.\n\n����������� Timid engineer Chris (David Arquette) is returning to ill-named Prosperity, Arizona, hoping to revive romance with Sheriff Samantha (Kari Wuhrer), and reopen his father’s old mine. The resourceful sheriff’s a single mom to spider-crazy 12-year-old Mike (Scott Terra) and teen Ashley (ScarlettJohansson).\n\n����������� Mike discovers that the creepy-crawly residents of a local spider museum are mutating after feeding on toxic waste, tripling in size by the hour. And they are hungry.\n\n����������� Soon the alarming arachnids are leapfrogging after terrified teen dirt bikers and pouncing on the local ostriches (don’t ask). Once they reach town and wreak deadly havoc, the sheriff summons all survivors to the supposed safety of the enclosed mall. With the phone lines out it’s up to Chris and Mike to save the day while the sheriff and townspeople stave off the murderous marauders with everything from pitchforks to perfume, which confuses the sensitive critters.\n\n����������� Director Ellory Elkayem� maintains a feeble comic tone in this spoofy throwback to the sci-fi atomic horror films of the 1950s like “Tarantula” and “Them!” This makes the fantasy violence seem more silly than nasty, but the derivative movie is never effectively scary or suspenseful. The amusement factor is small while the plot’s sheer predictability looms large. Combine that with Arquette’s acting range - - from expressionless to mugging - - and you have an instantly forgettable film.\n\n����������� The real stars of the movie of course are the various sized spiders which are computer-generated then wedded to the rest of the film for all the attack scenes. The super-sized spiders are daunting at first but less so with each succeeding, as in repetitive, chase after the fleeing humans.\n\nNovice director Elkayemdoesn’t know when to pull back, stretching out the action scenes until they lose their punch and just end up being annoying. Doug E. Doug tries for laughs as the town’s paranoid deejay and Tom Noonan plays the eccentric and doomed spider museum owner. Wuhrer as the shapelysheriff, and Terra as her son are smartly appealing.\n\n����������� If spiders exploding with goppy green gooisn’t your cup of tea, you too may hope that “Eight Legged Freaks” doesn’t have - - as they say in the business to indicate longevity - - “legs.”\n\n����������� Due to recurring fantasy violence, fleeting sexual innuendo and a few crass expressions, the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-II -- adults and adolescents. The Motion Picture Association of America rating is PG-13 -- parents are strongly cautioned. Some material may be inappropriate for children under 13. ���\n\n-----------------\n\n����������� Pare is director of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops' Office for Film and Broadcasting."
}